WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
There are several situations where you should always call 911 immediately.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.960
This includes if someone is unresponsive, experiencing chest discomfort, or showing

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.320
signs of a stroke.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.240
You should also call for severe injuries like deep burns or heavy bleeding, seizures, electric

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.879
shocks, or if someone has been exposed to poison.

00:00:19.879 --> 00:00:24.840
Anytime there is a problem with breathing, a suspected head, neck, or back injury, a

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:31.080
possible broken bone, or complications related to pregnancy, 911 is your first priority.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.680
This also applies if you see blood in someone's stool or vomit.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.799
When you get a dispatcher on the line, be sure to give them your exact location and

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:41.279
answer all of their questions clearly.

00:00:41.279 --> 00:00:44.040
Don't worry about the questions slowing things down.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.000
Answering them actually helps help get to you faster.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.520
Try to put your phone on speaker so you can keep your hands free to provide care while

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.919
following the dispatcher's instructions.

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:57.439
It is a great idea to memorize the addresses of places you visit often, like your office

00:00:57.439 --> 00:01:01.560
or a gym, so you can give that information instantly during an emergency.

